    "summary": "Monster first set and soaring Truckin’ > The Other One > Morning Dew",
    "blurb": "The May 26 1972 London show delivers an expansive 19‑song first set that rolls straight into a marathon second‑set suite. Highlights include the legendary Truckin’ → The Other One → Morning Dew sequence, tight rhythm work, and standout Pigpen organ moments. Crystal‑clear soundboard recordings make this performance a must‑listen for any Deadhead.",
    "review": "The Strand Lyceum on May 26 1972 finds the Grateful Dead firing on all cylinders, weaving together an almost four‑hour marathon that feels like two shows in one. The first set bursts open with tight renditions of classics—Cumberland Blues, Dire Wolf and a rolling Playing In The Band—that build momentum without missing a beat, while the China Cat Sunflower → I Know You Rider segue sets up the high‑energy groove for the closing Not Fade Away medley. \n\nThe second set is where the night truly soars: Truckin’ launches into an inspired jam that morphs seamlessly into The Other One, punctuated by a scorching drum solo and a soaring piano lead from Keith that has been likened to Steve Winwood. Garcia’s tone is incandescent throughout, especially during the climactic Morning Dew, which he delivers with emotional intensity that still gives listeners chills. Pigpen adds powerful organ colors early in the show, and Bill Kreutzmann’s percussive drive keeps the momentum relentless. The set closes on a high note with Sing Me Back Home, Me And My Uncle, and a jubilant one‑song encore of One More Saturday Night.",
